; Tutorial 1.06: Arrays
;
; An array is a row of variables that share one name. Each one is found by its number, the "index". Arrays hold
; things like a high score table, the cells of a game board or the pixels of a picture.
;
; Works on: the original Blitz3D and BlitzPlus, and BlitzLLVM.

Graphics 800, 600, 0, 2 ; A bigger window, so that everything printed fits.

; --- Dim arrays ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Dim makes an array that every function can use. Dim scores(4) makes FIVE elements, numbered 0 to 4: the number
; is the highest index, not the count. Many programs ignore element 0 and use 1 to 4.
Dim scores(4)
scores(0) = 120
scores(1) = 450
scores(2) = 80
scores(3) = 300
scores(4) = 220

; A For loop visits every element.
Local total = 0
Local i
For i = 0 To 4
	total = total + scores(i)
Next
Print "The five scores add up to " + total + "."

; Arrays can hold floats and text too, using the same suffixes as variables.
Dim names$(2)
names$(0) = "Ada"
names$(1) = "Bo"
names$(2) = "Cy"
For i = 0 To 2
	Print "Player " + (i + 1) + " is " + names$(i)
Next

; --- Sorting --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A simple sort: swap neighbours that are in the wrong order until none are. It is slow for big arrays, but easy
; to follow.
Local swapped, temp
Repeat
	swapped = False
	For i = 0 To 3
		If scores(i) < scores(i + 1)
			temp = scores(i)
			scores(i) = scores(i + 1)
			scores(i + 1) = temp
			swapped = True
		EndIf
	Next
Until Not swapped

Print
Print "High scores:"
For i = 0 To 4
	Print "  " + (i + 1) + ". " + scores(i)
Next

; --- Two dimensions -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; An array can have several dimensions, like the rows and columns of a board.
Const BOARD_W = 7
Const BOARD_H = 4
Dim board(BOARD_W, BOARD_H)

; Fill it with a checker pattern: 1 on dark squares, 0 on light ones.
Local x, y
For y = 0 To BOARD_H
	For x = 0 To BOARD_W
		board(x, y) = (x + y) Mod 2
	Next
Next

Print
Local rowText$
For y = 0 To BOARD_H
	rowText$ = ""
	For x = 0 To BOARD_W
		If board(x, y) Then rowText$ = rowText$ + "#" Else rowText$ = rowText$ + "."
	Next
	Print rowText$
Next

; --- Resizing ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Calling Dim again on the same array gives it a new size. This clears every element back to 0!
Dim scores(9)
Print
Print "After Dim scores(9), scores(0) is " + scores(0) + " and the array has 10 elements."

; --- Blitz arrays ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Square brackets make a fixed-size array that can be Local, Global or a Field of a Type (tutorial 1.08). Like
; Dim, the number is the highest index: [3] gives indexes 0, 1, 2 and 3. The size must be a constant, and these
; arrays can not be resized.
Local direction[3]
direction[0] = 10
direction[1] = 20
direction[2] = 30
Print "direction[] holds " + direction[0] + ", " + direction[1] + " and " + direction[2] + "."

Print
Print "Press any key to quit."
WaitKey
End
